---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { Timeline } from '../islands/Timeline.js';
---

<Base title="Timeline" description="Scrub through design eras and watch the same page morph.">
  <h1>Timeline</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    One settings page. Scrub the slider and watch it move through design history. Pick a
    different palette and the shapes stay put while every colour changes.
  </p>
  <Timeline client:load />
  <h2>What is actually changing</h2>
  <p>
    The slider sets one attribute, <code>data-era</code>, on the wrapper element. The palette
    select sets another, <code>data-palette</code>. Every token is a CSS custom property scoped to
    those attributes, so the morph is the browser resolving different variables, not React
    rendering different components.
  </p>
  <p>
    This is also why the dialog in the danger zone tab keeps its era: it renders back into that
    same wrapper rather than into <code>document.body</code>, where the variables would not reach
    it.
  </p>
</Base>
